**Read:** [Mobility particle size spectrometers — Wiedensohler and colleagues](../sources/Mobility-Particle-Size-Spectrometers-Wiedensohler.lingua#r_1W06WPADV43B98EF8NSW9RDCWM), stepping/scanning, operating observations and data structure; [Demand-controlled cleanroom filtration — Faulkner, Fisk and Walton](../sources/Demand-Controlled-Cleanroom-Filtration-Faulkner-Fisk-Walton.lingua#r_2W1ADE030W9RFA8ATDKSYVT6XP), sampling intervals and averages. Revisit [A control sensor observes one part of the facility](../facilities/A-control-sensor-observes-one-part-of-the-facility.lingua#r_7PMBE577HPDTS73C2SJVT73PH3).

A sequential scan measures different mobility settings at different times. Recovering one size distribution commonly assumes the population remained sufficiently stable during the scan. A changing process can therefore appear as a size feature if time and selected size are confounded.

Let a stationary shape n₀(d) be multiplied by a changing amplitude a(t). Even with ideal selection, a scan records values proportional to a(t_j)n₀(d_j). If amplitude doubles between the first and last channels, the apparent relative abundance of those sizes can double although the shape never changed. Scanning in reverse order or using a simultaneous total counter can help test this explanation, subject to their own response and loss models.

Transport lag, CPC response and averaging spread each observation across time. A first-order response to a short pulse may never reach the pulse maximum. Assigning a record's timestamp to its start, centre or end changes alignment with a process event.

**Work:** Generate a smooth two-size teaching population and vary only its amplitude during an ascending scan. Reconstruct the misleading distribution, then compare descending and repeated scans. Preserve raw settings, timing, flow/state observations and processing parameters. Explain which changes can be corrected from these data and which require a new measurement.

